{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And behold, YHWH stood over him.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): over the ladder. <b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: 'over him' — over Jacob. And Scripture likened Him to the cherub of which it is written (Psalms 18): ״וירכב על כרוב ויעוף״, for the patriarchs are themselves the merkavah, in the likeness of the cherubim above. You already know that Jacob is called 'small,' and the masters of truth said regarding the cherubim: large face and small face, and this is what David, peace be upon him, said by way of the Kabbalah (Psalms 104): ״חיות קטנות עם גדולות״. And I enlighten the eyes of your heart that the form of Jacob is engraved upon the Throne of Glory, and from this Scripture said (Genesis 25): ״יושב אהלים״ — the tents above and below. And a clarification of this you will find at the giving of the Torah (Exodus 24): ״ויראו את אלהי ישראל ותחת רגליו״ — it comes to allude to ישראל beneath His feet. And this is what is said in this parashah: ״התחת אלהים אנכי״ — not as a question. And understand this." "<b>I am YHWH, the Elohim of Abraham your father and the Elohim of Isaac.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): Scripture promises Jacob and his seed the gift of the land, and that He will be with him and guard him in all his ways, in the manner written concerning the virtue of the righteous (Psalms 91:11–12): ״כי מלאכיו יצוה לך לשמרך בכל דרכיך״. And concerning this He used for Himself the expression אני, in the manner of what is written (Numbers 18): ״אני חלקך ונחלתך״, to teach you that He is the portion of HaShem, in the manner of what is written (Deuteronomy 32): ״כי חלק ה' עמו יעקב חבל נחלתו״ — not the portion of the angels of Elohim that He mentioned." "<b>And behold.</b> This passage is a promise to Jacob for the time when the Omnipresent will be with him and prosper the path on which he walks, and that He will return him to his father's house in the Holy Land; also a promise to his seed for all generations, that He will be with them in every place to which they are exiled and enslaved among the kingdoms. As our rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: in every place they were exiled, the Shekhinah was with them — that He will guard them from the troubles that come upon them in exile, and that their enemies will not prevail over them, and that He will restore them to their land in the end. This is: ״והשבתיך אל האדמה הזאת כי לא אעזבך״, and likewise it is written (Ezra 9): ״ובעבדותנו לא עזבנו אלהינו״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: ״אני ה' אלהי אברהם אביך ואלהי יצחק״ — in this He mentioned the two attributes, Hesed and Gevurah. And this place was Beth El, of which Jacob said of it: ״אין זה כי אם בית אלהים״, and he called it Beth El; this is ״ויקרא את שם המקום ההוא בית אל והוא לוז״. And this is not the Beth El near Ai, for there were two places whose name was Beth El: one is this place, Beth El, which is Jerusalem; and the second is Beth El near Ai, in which Jeroboam set up one calf. And concerning these two attributes Scripture said: ״ויקרא למקום אל בית אל כי שם נגלו אליו האלהים״, and this verse does not come to obscure but to explain. And this is what is written (Joshua 24): ״אלהים קדושים״, (Psalms 58): ״אלהים שופטים״, and from this Onkelos translated: מה נורא מה דחילו — and did not translate מה דחיל in the singular. And concerning them it is written (Isaiah 6): ״שרפים עומדים ממעל לו״." "<b>And since</b> these two attributes were aligned against the Beth El where Jeroboam set up two golden calves opposite them — therefore he made two of the calves, for what Israel made was one: they sought a leader in the wilderness, and one leader of Israel was enough for them. But Jeroboam made two for this intention: one against Hesed, in order to draw from it and to cut off its power from the central line, which draws from the forces of Jerusalem; and the second against the attribute of Gevurah, for he wished to overpower the kingship himself and to draw the kingship to himself by the power of the attribute of Gevurah, which is the attribute of a king. And that he set it up outside Jerusalem — he intended by this to uproot the kingship from the House of David, because Jerusalem is called עיר צדק, and there is the power of the kingship of the House of David, which is the attribute of judgment, and that was the power and attribute of David, as it is said (Isaiah 32): ״הן לצדק ימלך מלך״. Also the Holy Temple is aligned opposite the Throne of Glory, and he wished to nullify the power of the kingship and its thrones of judgment, which are the throne of judgment and the throne of mercy. Therefore he deliberated deeply to depart from the place of the Holy Temple and the city of Jerusalem, as our rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״וירבעם יצא מירושלים״ (1 Kings 11) — from the border-region of Jerusalem. And these are the five lower ones, which are the forces of Jerusalem, from whose authority he removed himself. He elevated his thought and turned upward to Hesed and Gevurah to make two calves opposite them, and he uprooted the supernal kingship from its place of nurture above, in order to nullify and uproot the kingship from the House of David below. Behold, this was the sin of idolatry, for he cut through the plantings and separated what was unified. And from this the masters of truth called him 'a destroying man,' and assigned him to join with 'a robber of his father and mother,' for so he robbed from Tiferet and from Hod, the power above. From here it is explained that his cutting was between five and five. And this is what he said (1 Kings 12): ״רב לכם מעלות ירושלים הנה אלהיך ישראל אשר העלוך״ etc. — he says: why do you need the forces of Jerusalem? Here are your gods — Hesed and Gevurah — from which bounty comes to them. And behold, Jeroboam thereby transgressed (Exodus 20): ״לא תעשון אתי״ etc. And Scripture said (1 Kings 12): ״וישם את האחד בבית אל ואת האחד בדן״ — and this Beth El is not Jerusalem, but the other Beth El near Ai, as I mentioned above. <b>And</b> Jeroboam would mislead Israel in this and say to them that his intention was not idolatry at all, but a conjunction of the two attributes for the benefit of Israel. And he would show them a face as though his intention was not to prevent Israel from going up for the pilgrimage festivals, but out of fear lest they kill him — since there is no sitting in the courtyard except for kings of the House of David alone; if Israel goes up on pilgrimage and everyone sees Jeroboam standing and Rehoboam sitting, then they would kill Jeroboam, for they would not consider him a king. And this is what is written (ibid.): ״ויאמר ירבעם בלבו עתה תשוב הממלכה לבית דוד אם יעלה העם הזה לעשות זבחים בבית ה' בירושלים ושב לב העם הזה אל אדוניהם אל רחבעם מלך יהודה והרגוני ושבו אל רחבעם מלך יהודה״. And it is written: ״ויועץ המלך ויעש שני עגלי זהב ויאמר להם רב לכם״ etc. You already know that Amos prophesied calamity and punishment upon Beth El, and said that he saw the attribute of judgment seeking the destruction of Beth El and Dan, of which it is written (Amos 7:7–8): ״והנה ה' נצב על חומת אנך ובידו אנך״, and it is written: ״ויאמר הנני שם אנך בקרב עמי ישראל לא אוסיף עוד עבור לו״ — that is, I will no longer pass over his transgression. אנך is the line of judgment and justice decreed from the mouth of the heavenly court, for the numerical value of אנך is seventy-one. And it is written after it: ״ונשמו במות ישחק ומקדשי ישראל יחרבו וקמתי על בית ירבעם בחרב״ — and it should have said במות ירבעם, but the intention is: ונשמו במות ירבעם, which he made opposite the attribute of Isaac. And from this ישחק is written with a ש, for the letter ש is fire, and it alludes to the attribute of Gevurah." "<b>And likewise</b> in the Book of Formation (Sefer Yetzirah): מ\"ם silent, ש\"ן hissing. And further there: He crowned the ש\"ן with fire. And he said: 'My sanctuary upon Beit El and Dan' — and since Amos was prophesying this calamity upon Beit El, Amatziah, who was the priest of Beit El, came and gave him counsel that he should flee from there and not prophesy thus in Beit El, but rather in the Holy Temple (בהמ\"ק), because the prophecy was coming to uproot the sanctity from Beit El and add it to the Holy Temple (בהמ\"ק). This is what is said (Amos 7): ״לך ברח לך אל ארץ יהודה״ — this is the Holy Temple (בהמ\"ק); ״ואכל שם לחם ושם תנבא ובית אל לא תוסיף עוד להנבא כי מקדש מלך הוא ובית ממלכה הוא״ — מקדש מלך is the attribute of Gedullah, and בית ממלכה is the attribute of Gevurah. And this is what is alluded to here: אכ\"ן יש ה' — for its numerical value is that of אנ\"ך."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 28, :notes {1 "The author cites Psalms 91:11–12 (תהילים צ״א:י״ב-י״ב); the citation range normalized to 91:11–12.", 2 "The author writes הש״י, expanded to השם יתברך ('the Omnipresent' in standard English rendering of the epithet).", 5 "The phrase 'ואמר מקדשי על בית אל ודן' appears to be a paraphrastic allusion rather than a direct biblical quotation; no exact verse matching these words has been identified. The closing gematria אכ\"ן יש ה' = אנ\"ך (both = 91) is rendered exactly as written."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:16:22.929935203Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}